course: dictionary
lesson: 9335
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word spine has more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Look at the words around it. «The stonefish has venomous spines along its back» is about sharp, pointed parts, because venomous and back make you think of a sting, not a bone.
explain: «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage» is about your backbone, the row of small bones down the middle of your back. The ribs tell you it is a body part. Ask: does my meaning fit the sentence? If it does, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Spine can also mean the back edge of a book, where the title is printed, or courage, as in having the spine to do something. Phrases help too: «spine-chilling» means scary, and «shivers down my spine» means you feel a sudden fear.
fact: A spine is the row of small bones down the middle of your back, also called the backbone.
fact: The spine protects the spinal cord, which carries messages between the brain and the body.
fact: Animals with a backbone are vertebrates, such as fish, birds and mammals. Animals without one are invertebrates, such as insects, worms and jellyfish.
fact: A spine is also a sharp, pointed part on an animal or plant, like the spines of a hedgehog or a cactus.
fact: On a book, the spine is the back edge where the pages are joined and the title is usually printed, so you can find the book on a shelf.
exercise: «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage.» What is the spine here? | A sharp part on a cactus | The back edge of a book | Courage to do something hard | *A row of small bones down your back
exercise: «The stonefish has venomous spines along its back.» What does spines mean here? | Soft, fluffy hairs | Bones inside your chest | Pages in a book | *Sharp, pointed parts
exercise: Which sentence uses spine to mean courage? | *He had the spine to stand up and speak to the whole class | The title was printed on the spine of the book | Her spine hurt after the long run | A hedgehog curled up, covered in spines
test: «Spine-chilling» means what? | Wet and muddy | Very cold, like ice | *Scary | Funny and silly
test: Why does a book have a spine? | To hide the pictures inside | *To keep the pages joined and show the title on a shelf | To stop the book from being opened | To make the book heavier
exam: Which animal has a backbone? | Worm | *Fish | Slug | Jellyfish
